 a  certain amount of time  and whenever this time has not elapsed  continue using https when you talk to me  um that's neat right so the browser will  now  always use https but what about this one  it's plain text again what is what if  there's a network attacker that is  basically just  stripping off the forward to https  and what if i tell you there are a lot  of tools out there which  are really easy to use which are just  doing that they set up a proxy  everything is going through for you fine  and the user isn't actually using https  if they are again for example in the  coffee shop  so to fill even this gap there is a  preload list in modern browsers and  actually firefox and chrome basically  share the implementation of the list and  synchronize  and this preload list basically contains  the bit here um  let's cache this kind of thing so the  browser will always use https without  requiring the first  strict transport security header but  this list we have in the browsers  well they are opt-in because we can't  force other web applications into  supporting https of course  so to fill this list um a security  engineer  adam langley working at google on https  things went around conferences security  conferences and other conferences and  said  hey people do you want to be on that  list it's awesome it's secure it's  better than what you have right now  just send me an email and you know this  method to maintain  to maintain the list it scaled because  nobody sent him an email  um yeah you're laughing i find that  actually rather sad  so until just last month when they set  up a google form so  i hope the mails coming in have either  increased or you just got bored  maintaining the list manually  but now there's a google form you can do  it in a more automated way  but yeah still if you're using web  applications and if you're providing  them to somebody  and if you're already using https please  consider  using strict transport security in your  headers as we