at looks like an attack for  example a script tag that has been  smuggled into the web page  the filter will say hey this script is  exactly what has been in the response  and i'm pretty sure this is an attack  so i'll remove it or modify it so it  isn't really passed  as a script and the user's secure which  is fine  but what if there was some static html  or javascript that is in your web page  which somebody fakes as if it was in the  response but doesn't have any value  for the web application basically making  up a fake parameter  and sending it to the server the server  doesn't really work with it it just  ignores it because it's unknown and fake  but the browser will think hey this is  the same i'm going to remove it  yeah so what if this was a security  feature huh  so the solution to all these problems is  basically disallow framing  you probably don't want your website to  be framed why would you  um the key is to just send a header with  all your http responses that says x  frame options  deny if you do need framing sometimes  you can also pass in different other  options that might be useful for me  allow from  or same origin and luckily you all know  what same origin means now right  so this framing thing um is basically a  security problem we've been having for  12 years  the first concerns were raised in 2002  the header itself was implemented in  2008 but the rfc was actually only  published last year  and there is much much more an evil  person could do to your website and to  the users of your website  if you allow it to be displayed in the  frame i actually wrote a very long paper  and a blog post with a friend of mine  about it  um there would be link in the slides  when i published them so if you're  interested in that  kind of nifty list of attacks i  recommend you look into that  other than that i want to jump forward  into the future of browser security  features  i will have to talk about frames again  um i also want to mention cross-site  s